Troubleshooting
Symptom: A pipeline fails immediately with groovy.lang.MissingPropertyException: No such property: BRANCH_NAME. Diagnostic hint: env.BRANCH_NAME is only populated for Multibranch Pipeline jobs, not standard Pipeline jobs; check which job type you created and adjust the script to use env.BUILD_NUMBER logic instead, as shown in Subtask 3.1.
Symptom: The parallel block in Subtask 4.2 runs both branches but the build still shows only a single sequential timeline in older Jenkins UI themes. Diagnostic hint: confirm the Pipeline plugin and Jenkins core are up to date, and check the "Console Output" (not just Stage View) for interleaved log lines from both branches to confirm true parallel execution.
Conclusion
In this lab, you installed Jenkins from scratch and built a series of scripted pipelines that progressed from basic node/stage syntax to advanced techniques including branching logic, retry-and-rollback error handling, dynamically generated stages driven by Groovy collections, reusable pipeline functions, and parallel test execution with archived reporting. These exercises demonstrate the flexibility that scripted pipelines offer over declarative syntax when a workflow needs genuine programming constructs such as loops over service lists or functions shared across stages. To continue building on these skills, explore Jenkins Shared Libraries to move reusable functions like buildService and runQualityCheck into a version-controlled library usable across multiple jobs, investigate webhook-triggered builds so pipelines start automatically on Git push events, and study pipeline performance optimization techniques such as parallelizing independent stages and using lightweight executors to reduce overall build time.
